A `@run` handler that reads no state is called the way it was emitted.

The build makes the handler a function of the program's, and a function that
touches no state is emitted free on the Go targets rather than as a Model
method. Each host's main spelled the call `m.__run(…)` regardless, against a
file declaring `func __run`, and the program did not compile.

deny out/go/gtk4/callbacks.go `m\.__run` -- __run is free here
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `m\.__run` -- likewise

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        gtk4(@run(args, run) {
            if args.length > 0 {
                run()
            }
        })
        fyne(@run(args, run) {
            run()
        })
    }
}

ui.window {
    ui.text(value="hi")
}
-- out/go/fyne/model.go --
